\DOC LOCAL_NSQR_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_NSQR_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a subroutine call in an {eventually} symbolic-execution goal by
applying a named specification assumption, advancing the goal from the state
before the call to the state after it.

\DESCRIBE
{assump_name : string} names the assumption of the goal that holds the callee's
{ensures} specification to be used, and {n : int} is the index of the symbolic
state at the call site, from which the successor state name is formed. The goal
is an {eventually} statement about state {sn}, and the result is a single
subgoal of the same shape about {s(n+1)}, the conclusion being otherwise
unchanged. The assumptions are rewritten: facts about the pre-call state {sn} —
its program counter, stack pointer, argument registers, event trace, memory
reads, accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation and any flag assumptions — are removed,
and facts about {s(n+1)} are added: a new program counter, the extended event
trace written as an {APPEND} of a fresh event variable onto the old trace, two
new assumptions about that fresh event variable (one equating it to the
callee's event function applied to the actual arguments, one asserting its
memory accesses are in bounds), the surviving memory and register reads, and an
enlarged {MAYCHANGE} relation. The named specification assumption survives,
moved to the end of the list.
{
   A ?- eventually ... sn
   ================================================  LOCAL_NSQR_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n
   A' ?- eventually ... s(n+1)
}
\noindent where {A'} replaces the state-{sn} facts by the state-{s(n+1)} facts
and adds the two new assumptions about the fresh event variable.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal behaviour on goals of this form.

\ENDDOC
